#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128768 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "344f1838-a027-5fd5-82da-17e9b4fcac2b" :knowledge-point/title "Further Translating Problems With Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "b94f1140-03a8-541b-9401-7456412fcd92" :question/math-academy-id "e-10071" :question/problem "The area $A$ of a forest decreases with time due to deforestation. The rate of change of the forest area is proportional to the square of the area. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the area at time $t?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **area** $A$ of a forest **decreases** with time due to deforestation. The **rate** of change of the forest area is **proportional** to the **area squared**.  *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}$ is *proportional to* the *area squared.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} \\propto A^2.\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\propto$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the area *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = -kA^2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128769 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d6e417b-e2a7-57f1-8dcd-4de5ff5d7e6b" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Inversely Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "3c8ddbe7-4a53-5340-bab4-1ffdb602e155" :question/math-academy-id "e-10073" :question/problem "The volume of water $V$ in a bucket decreases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the square of the volume of water. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the volume of water $V?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The  **volume of water** $V$  in a bucket **decreases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the ** square of the volume of water** $V.$*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume of water* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *square of the volume.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} \\propto \\dfrac{1}{V^2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\propto$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the volume of water *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} = -\\dfrac{k}{V^2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128871 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a247a205-ca5c-5079-820c-cfa34b6f53db" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Constant Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "725a3b25-e8ad-501f-9b32-3f4bc42e2c8e" :question/math-academy-id "e-521" :question/problem "A helium balloon loses $20$ grams of helium every second. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the mass $M$ of the balloon?" :question/worked-solution "If the mass of the balloon is $M$ grams, then the rate of change of the mass is $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}M}{\\textrm{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in seconds.\n\nSince $20$ grams are lost every second, the rate of change of the balloon's mass is given by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}M}{\\textrm{d}t}=-20.\n$$\n\nNotice that the right-hand side is *negative* because the balloon is *losing* mass."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128872 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "255dee12-5f44-536b-b679-5a894843395d" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "2f48bc21-3bd4-5dd1-8b60-89121591bfed" :question/math-academy-id "e-522" :question/problem "The volume of water $V(t)$ in a leaking tank decreases at a rate that is proportional to the time $t$ (in minutes) since the leak started. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the volume of water at time $t?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of water $V(t)$ in a leaking tank **decreases** at a **rate** that is **proportional** to the **time** $t$ (in minutes) since the leak started. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume* of water varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}$ is *proportional to* the *time* since the water begins to leak.  In other words,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} \\propto t.\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\propto$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the volume of water *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} = -kt.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295874 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "344f1838-a027-5fd5-82da-17e9b4fcac2b" :knowledge-point/title "Further Translating Problems With Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a4c60a8-f101-5eef-9c93-587751322eb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=k\\sqrt{v},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295876} {:answer/id #uuid "63a1febe-6a7a-5422-8c17-c5540c2e7e97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-k{v}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295877} {:answer/id #uuid "9188262e-9673-5eed-83b2-a5fd5cd3b6d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=k{v}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295878} {:answer/id #uuid "7f71007e-d2c5-54ea-bfc6-348221a38aa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{{v}^{2}},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295879} {:answer/id #uuid "114d7be1-1926-558d-8724-31e887cac167" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{v}^{2}},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63a1febe-6a7a-5422-8c17-c5540c2e7e97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-k{v}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295877} :answer-field/id #uuid "27ffd44f-d95c-5b66-9de7-8b47da4773de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "000bf12e-faf9-50f0-869d-67748f8e8824" :question/math-academy-id "q-143036" :question/problem "The velocity $v$ of a stone falling through some water to the bottom of a lake decreases with time. The rate of change of the stone's velocity is proportional to the square of the velocity. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the velocity at time $t?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **velocity** $v$ of a stone falling through some water to the bottom of a lake **decreases** with time. The **rate** of change of the stone's velocity is **proportional** to the **square of the velocity**.  *\n\nWe note the following: \n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *velocity* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *velocity squared.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}∝{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the velocity *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-k{v}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295881 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d6e417b-e2a7-57f1-8dcd-4de5ff5d7e6b" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Inversely Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aefdcf67-bcdc-5ed9-b0c0-b392a37658bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{H},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295883} {:answer/id #uuid "08fff3d5-f006-5e51-b487-252567288d43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-kH,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295884} {:answer/id #uuid "0cb91c8b-41a8-5201-a728-dda49efb372f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=kH,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295885} {:answer/id #uuid "d0e1903b-8dd1-5d3e-990b-7e0192bf2bf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{t},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295886} {:answer/id #uuid "ce285e3c-2f8f-5986-9c6a-2c74be616204" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{H},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aefdcf67-bcdc-5ed9-b0c0-b392a37658bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{H},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295883} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9392e9a-f656-5daf-b678-018e74b9f8bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295882}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "91ea1276-29dd-5d34-8bf4-312e105d7100" :question/math-academy-id "q-143058" :question/problem "The height $H$ of a tree increases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the tree's height at that moment.\nWhat differential equation describes the rate of change of the height $H?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **height** $H$ of a tree **increases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the **tree's height** $H$ at that moment. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *height* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *tree's height.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}∝\\frac{1}{H}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the height *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{H}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281759 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d6e417b-e2a7-57f1-8dcd-4de5ff5d7e6b" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Inversely Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4cf587d-db87-57e5-9a06-a4b3afa3e174" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{t},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281761} {:answer/id #uuid "b5d87430-9de6-50b0-a49f-c9df03b20297" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=kt,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281762} {:answer/id #uuid "6574d030-f380-59b8-aa8b-254c8f43b957" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{C},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281763} {:answer/id #uuid "042ca691-6da5-522d-9381-8c694df7c921" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=-kC,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281764} {:answer/id #uuid "c1bc5056-30f6-5a25-a4fb-b5a739b891e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{t},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1bc5056-30f6-5a25-a4fb-b5a739b891e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{t},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281765} :answer-field/id #uuid "35de313a-7ad5-57b8-9455-ebbcd948f70c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281760}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f6d27ee8-7a9e-59cc-aba5-99cecf01eb5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-143081" :question/problem "The charge $C$ of a phone battery decreases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the time $t$ spent charging the smartphone. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the charge $C$ of the phone battery?" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **charge** $C$ of a phone battery **decreases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the **time** $t$ spent charging the smartphone.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *charge* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *time.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}∝\\frac{1}{t}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the number *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{t}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281766 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d6e417b-e2a7-57f1-8dcd-4de5ff5d7e6b" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Inversely Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab0a2484-9701-5ebf-9683-ac60974c8b6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=-k{t}^{2},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281768} {:answer/id #uuid "9160920f-9320-5c23-a35b-5ff41cbc2b71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{{S}^{2}},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281769} {:answer/id #uuid "9dcd7858-cbe0-5076-8b17-8092abaf53a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{t},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281770} {:answer/id #uuid "8fbac362-195b-5db7-b1fb-57506ad7b25e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{S}^{2}},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281771} {:answer/id #uuid "3815e13d-52cc-566d-8290-274d1e7a3f3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{t}^{2}},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3815e13d-52cc-566d-8290-274d1e7a3f3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{t}^{2}},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281772} :answer-field/id #uuid "088bf1a8-a840-5d18-8346-7195ea4d0130" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d4f83b6d-0b59-5cc8-9341-b97fb9c62549" :question/math-academy-id "q-143094" :question/problem "The speed $S$ of an object decreases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the square of the travel time $t.$ What differential equation describes the rate of change of the speed $S?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **speed** $S$ of an object **decreases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the **square of the travel time** $t.$*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *speed* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *square of the travel time.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}∝\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the speed *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{t}^{2}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281777 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a247a205-ca5c-5079-820c-cfa34b6f53db" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Constant Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa989acd-9827-50f5-abe0-a1c87b056cb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa989acd-9827-50f5-abe0-a1c87b056cb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281779} :answer-field/id #uuid "67e81cb6-0174-51cd-af4e-179781d14adb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "01d0d014-2d16-55f2-87e9-0d2babf92850" :question/math-academy-id "q-353449" :question/problem "The number of completed online course enrollments increases by $18$ enrollments every hour during a promotional event. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the number of enrollments $E(t)?$ Assume that the time $t$ is measured in hours.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}E}{\\text{d}t}=$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If the number of enrollments is $E,$ then the rate of change of the number of enrollments is $\\frac{\\text{d}E}{\\text{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in hours.\n\nSince $18$ new enrollments are added every hour, the rate of change of the number of enrollments is given by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}E}{\\text{d}t}=18.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295894 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "255dee12-5f44-536b-b679-5a894843395d" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3fc1e2a-d7b9-55f3-a8db-4948caf4a01d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=tA" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295896} {:answer/id #uuid "ee7dacc6-1e01-5aed-9065-745a0c1b937c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295897} {:answer/id #uuid "67be357a-be3d-5b48-8131-6c31c67bb821" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=k+A,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295898} {:answer/id #uuid "447c66c6-c98e-5588-85c8-7e7329e54077" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=k-A,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295899} {:answer/id #uuid "26ae4d63-2052-5085-b656-7698d7989d9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=kA,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295900}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26ae4d63-2052-5085-b656-7698d7989d9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=kA,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295900} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ebaaa1d-ab07-54c5-a725-cf8b3eccfce2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6e3c5c93-86d8-5789-925d-7bd84acb1b9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-38588" :question/problem "The number of ants in an anthill increases at a rate that is proportional to the number of ants at the moment. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the number of ants, $A,$ at time $t?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **number of ants** in an anthill **increases** at a **rate** that is **proportional** to the **number of ants** at the moment.* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *number of ants* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *number of ants.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}∝A.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the number of ants *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=kA.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281787 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "255dee12-5f44-536b-b679-5a894843395d" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00d87778-9f6d-572f-ba24-efb2b01987c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k+V,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281789} {:answer/id #uuid "12cc5a2f-4920-5e47-b94d-215b9f53fb7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=kV,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281790} {:answer/id #uuid "7879ec9f-de30-5298-b5ad-eb700ebabf2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=tV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281791} {:answer/id #uuid "32a89d93-5177-5162-bd49-ea2e6085aa05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k-V,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281792} {:answer/id #uuid "999d8f50-9a49-5221-87cf-61d7ddd7de1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=V" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281793}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12cc5a2f-4920-5e47-b94d-215b9f53fb7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=kV,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281790} :answer-field/id #uuid "d75ca6da-7fe4-5e39-8c9d-57a413cefe1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281788}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1d093bd1-c5c7-53da-bfe8-604b0e2c79b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-38589" :question/problem "Some people in a community contracted an unknown virus. The number of people infected by the virus increases at a rate that is proportional to the number of people infected by the virus. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the number of people who contracted the virus, $V,$ at time $t?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **number of people** infected by the virus **increases** at a **rate** that is **proportional** to the **number of people** infected by the virus.* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *number of people* infected by the virus varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *number of people* infected. In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}∝V.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the number of people infected by the virus  *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=kV.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129749 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "344f1838-a027-5fd5-82da-17e9b4fcac2b" :knowledge-point/title "Further Translating Problems With Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2833a79-c64c-58e2-841e-294e5d5933dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dV}{dt} =-k\\sqrt{V}, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128289} {:answer/id #uuid "ee45cbbe-91ad-5e3a-b2b0-082969f6a84e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dV}{dt} = \\frac{k}{V^{2}}, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128290} {:answer/id #uuid "416f07cf-1706-50fa-8b17-6daf757e4753" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dV}{dt} =-kV^{2}, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128291} {:answer/id #uuid "545a0df9-1298-5759-a415-bd983883c823" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dV}{dt} =-kV, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128292} {:answer/id #uuid "70350c1e-f017-56cc-afb3-45ef43d59f4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dV}{dt} = kV^{2}, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128293}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "416f07cf-1706-50fa-8b17-6daf757e4753" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dV}{dt} =-kV^{2}, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128291} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1d3821f-04cc-5ace-979c-d8f8b97466ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128288}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d2a8b5ef-a26c-5387-8984-3108c19f3b8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-51247" :question/problem "The value $V$ of a particular car decreases with time. The rate of change of the car's value is proportional to the square of the value. What differential equation describes the change of the value at time $t?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **value** $V$ of a particular car **decreases** with time. The **rate** of change of the car's value is **proportional** to the **value squared**.  *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *value* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *value squared.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}∝{V}^{2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the value *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-k{V}^{2}.\n$$"}]}
